NNAAMMEE
       EVP_KDF-KRB5KDF - The RFC3961 Krb5 KDF EVP_KDF implementation

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Support for computing the KKRRBB55KKDDFF KDF through the EEVVPP__KKDDFF API.

       The EVP_KDF-KRB5KDF algorithm implements the key derivation function
       defined in RFC 3961, section 5.1 and is used by Krb5 to derive session
       keys.  Three inputs are required to perform key derivation: a cipher,
       (for example AES-128-CBC), the initial key, and a constant.

   IIddeennttiittyy
       "KRB5KDF" is the name for this implementation; it can be used with the
       EEVVPP__KKDDFF__ffeettcchh(()) function.

   SSuuppppoorrtteedd ppaarraammeetteerrss
       The supported parameters are:

       "properties" (OOSSSSLL__KKDDFF__PPAARRAAMM__PPRROOPPEERRTTIIEESS) <UTF8 string>
       "cipher" (OOSSSSLL__KKDDFF__PPAARRAAMM__CCIIPPHHEERR) <UTF8 string>
       "key" (OOSSSSLL__KKDDFF__PPAARRAAMM__KKEEYY) <octet string>
           These parameters work as described in "PARAMETERS" in EEVVPP__KKDDFF(3).

       "constant" (OOSSSSLL__KKDDFF__PPAARRAAMM__CCOONNSSTTAANNTT) <octet string>
           This  parameter sets the constant value for the KDF.  If a value is
           already set, the contents are replaced.

NNOOTTEESS
       A context for KRB5KDF can be obtained by calling:

        EVP_KDF *kdf = EVP_KDF_fetch(NULL, "KRB5KDF", NULL);
        EVP_KDF_CTX *kctx = EVP_KDF_CTX_new(kdf);

       The output length of the KRB5KDF derivation is specified via the _k_e_y_l_e_n
       parameter to the EEVVPP__KKDDFF__ddeerriivvee(3) function, and  MUST  match  the  key
       length  for  the  chosen  cipher or an error is returned. Moreover, the
       constant’s length must not exceed the block size of the cipher.   Since
       the  KRB5KDF  output  length  depends  on  the  chosen  cipher, calling
       EEVVPP__KKDDFF__CCTTXX__ggeett__kkddff__ssiizzee(3) to obtain the requisite length returns  the
       correct   length   only   after  the  cipher  is  set.  Prior  to  that
       EEVVPP__MMAAXX__KKEEYY__LLEENNGGTTHH is returned.  The caller must allocate a  buffer  of
       the  correct  length for the chosen cipher, and pass that buffer to the
       EEVVPP__KKDDFF__ddeerriivvee(3) function along with that length.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       This example derives a key using the AES-128-CBC cipher:

        EVP_KDF *kdf;
        EVP_KDF_CTX *kctx;
        unsigned char key[16] = "01234...";
        unsigned char constant[] = "I'm a constant";
        unsigned char out[16];
        size_t outlen = sizeof(out);
        OSSL_PARAM params[4], *p = params;

        kdf = EVP_KDF_fetch(NULL, "KRB5KDF", NULL);
        kctx = EVP_KDF_CTX_new(kdf);
        EVP_KDF_free(kdf);

        *p++ = OSSL_PARAM_construct_utf8_string(OSSL_KDF_PARAM_CIPHER,
                                                SN_aes_128_cbc,
                                                strlen(SN_aes_128_cbc));
        *p++ = OSSL_PARAM_construct_octet_string(OSSL_KDF_PARAM_KEY,
                                                 key, (size_t)16);
        *p++ = OSSL_PARAM_construct_octet_string(OSSL_KDF_PARAM_CONSTANT,
                                                 constant, strlen(constant));
        *p = OSSL_PARAM_construct_end();
        if (EVP_KDF_derive(kctx, out, outlen, params) <= 0)
            /* Error */

        EVP_KDF_CTX_free(kctx);

